<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hephaestus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hḗphaistos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hephaestus is the Greek god of artisans, blacksmiths, craftsmen, fire, metalworking, sculpture and volcanoes. In Greek mythology he was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, either on her own, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a>, or by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and he was cast off Mount Olympus because of his lameness, by Hera in one account and by Zeus in another. As the blacksmith of the gods he made all their weapons, and his symbols are the smith's hammer, the anvil and a pair of tongs. His cult was based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, and he was worshipped in the manufacturing centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> name found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> probably attests his worship. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he was equated with Vulcan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hephaestus.jpg" alt="Hephaestus" width="330" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Foundry Painter (name vase) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hephaistos_Thetis_at_Kylix_by_the_Foundry_Painter_Antikensammlung_Berlin_F2294.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> inscription A-pa-i-ti-jo, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, is believed to read a name derived from his, which would show his worship at that time, and the name Hēphaistos is most likely of Pre-Greek origin. Some sources hold that he began as a daemon of fire coming up from the earth, and that only later was a volcano taken for his smithy. His favourite place in the mortal world was the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, where he lived among the Sintians. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Iliad he is the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s Theogony Hera bore him alone, in revenge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>'s fathering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> without her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In one account <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> ejected him from the heavens because of his congenital impairment, and he was raised by Thetis and the Oceanid Eurynome. In another <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> flung him down for protecting Hera, and he fell for a day onto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, where the Sintians taught him to be a master craftsman. In an archaic story he took revenge on Hera with a golden throne that held her fast, and refused to return to Olympus until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> made him drunk and brought him back on a mule. On Olympus he had a workshop with an anvil and twenty bellows, and made the Aegis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a>'s winged helmet and sandals, and Pandora.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>His cult was based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a> and he was worshipped in the manufacturing centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Pausanias saw a painting in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> in Athens of Dionysus bringing Hephaestus up to heaven, and the return of Hephaestus was popular among Attic vase-painters, whose wares were favoured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>. Homer says he built automatons, including tripods that moved on golden wheels and golden handmaidens with understanding and speech. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> he sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and forged the armour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>. His symbols are the smith's hammer, the anvil and tongs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hephaestus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of grain; Athena, goddess of wisdom, war and crafts; Apollo, god of prophecy, music and healing; his sister Artemis, the huntress; Ares, god of war; Aphrodite, goddess of love; <b>Hephaestus</b>, the smith; Hermes, the messenger; and Hestia, goddess of the hearth, or Dionysus, god of wine and ecstasy.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>In grief Achilles returns to battle in new armour made by <b>Hephaestus</b>, its shield bearing an image of the stars at its centre, kills Hector and drags his body behind his chariot.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
